===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱel- (cover)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|helle}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hell}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hallju}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*haljō|t=concealed place, netherworld}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱel-|t=to cover, conceal, save}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Hälle|t=hell}}, {{cog|fy|hel|t=hell}}, {{cog|nl|hel|t=hell}}, {{cog|nds-de|Hell|t=hell}}, {{cog|de|Hölle|t=hell}}, {{cog|no|helvete|t=hell}}, {{cog|is|hel|t=the abode of the dead, death}}. Also related to the {{m|en|Hel}} of [[Germanic]] [[mythology]]. See also {{l|en|hele}}.

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|in many religions|uncountable}} A place of [[torment]] where some or all [[sinners]] and [[evil]] [[spirits]] are believed to go after [[death]].
#: {{ux|en|May you rot in '''hell'''!}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Proverbs|23|14|text=Thou shalt beat him with the rod, and shalt deliver his soul from '''hell'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1667|author=John Milton|title=Paradise Lost|passage=Better to reign in '''Hell''' than serve in '''Heaven'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Congreve Mourning Bride|page=39|passage=Heav'n has no Rage, like Love to Hatred turn'd, / Nor '''Hell''' a Fury, like a Woman ſcorn'd.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1916|author=James Joyce|title=Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man|passage='''Hell''' is a strait and dark and foul-smelling prison, an abode of demons and lost souls, filled with fire and smoke.}}

